{"id": 2717, "date": "2015-04-04T01:00:15", "date_gmt": "2015-04-04T05:00:15", "guid": {"rendered": "http://ravencypresswood.com/?p=2717"}, "modified": "2025-05-12T16:52:37", "modified_gmt": "2025-05-12T20:52:37", "slug": "the-field-of-accumulation-the-lineage-of-the-three-renowned-cycles-of-dzogchen", "status": "publish", "type": "post", "link": "https://ravencypresswood.com/2015/04/04/the-field-of-accumulation-the-lineage-of-the-three-renowned-cycles-of-dzogchen/", "title": {"rendered": "The Field of Accumulation: The Lineage of the Three-fold Cycle of Dzogchen Proclamations"}, "content": {"rendered": "<div id=\"attachment_2536\" style=\"width: 615px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-2536\" data-attachment-id=\"2536\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2014/12/06/the-field-of-accumulation-abbots-of-menri-monastery/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?fit=1580%2C1976&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1580,1976\" data-comments-opened=\"0\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"Tsok zhing according to Menri\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?fit=819%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"  wp-image-2536\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/tsok-zhing-according-to-menri.jpg?resize=605%2C756&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"Tsok zhing according to Menri\" width=\"605\" height=\"756\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-2536\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Tsok zhing according to MenriThe Yungdrung Bon Merit Field of the Menri Tradition</p></div>\n<p>In the Yungdrung Bön tradition, the place where the deities and objects of veneration are gathered is called the <span style=\"color: #000080\"><strong><em>&#8220;Tsok Zhing&#8221;, </em></strong></span>the <span style=\"color: #000080\"><strong>&#8220;Field of Accumulation&#8221;. </strong> </span>It is also sometimes translated as the <strong><span style=\"color: #000080\">&#8220;Field of Merit&#8221;</span></strong> or the <span style=\"color: #000080\"><strong>&#8220;Merit Field&#8221;</strong></span> because what is being accumulated by paying homage and making offerings to this place is merit, or virtue.  This is the Merit Field according to the Menri Tradition from a drawing done by the great master and scholar HE Yongdzin Tenzin Namdak Rinpoche.</p>\n<div id=\"attachment_2726\" style=\"width: 518px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-dzogchen-drakpa-korsum.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-2726\" data-attachment-id=\"2726\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2015/04/04/the-field-of-accumulation-the-lineage-of-the-three-renowned-cycles-of-dzogchen/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-dzogchen-drakpa-korsum/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-dzogchen-drakpa-korsum.jpg?fit=1166%2C1556&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"1166,1556\" data-comments-opened=\"0\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"Outline guide for tsok zhing Dzogchen Drakpa Korsum\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-dzogchen-drakpa-korsum.jpg?fit=767%2C1024&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"  wp-image-2726\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/outline-guide-for-tsok-zhing-dzogchen-drakpa-korsum.jpg?resize=508%2C678&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"Outline guide for tsok zhing Dzogchen Drakpa Korsum\" width=\"508\" height=\"678\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-2726\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Diagram highlighting the placement of the Dzogchen Lineage within the Merit Field thangkha.</p></div>\n<p>#1-28 depicts the <span style=\"color: #000080\"><strong>Lineage of the Three-fold Cycle of Dzogchen Proclamations</strong>.</span> In Tibetan, the <span style=\"color: #333399\"><strong><em>Dzogchen Drakpa Korsum Gyü</em></strong></span>.</p>\n<p>1. The <span style=\"color: #000080\">source of the lineage</span>, the White Light Deity, <span style=\"color: #000080\">Shenlha Ökar</span>.  He appears in the form of <em>dzok ku</em>, the enlightened Body of the Perfected Qualities and wears the corresponding ornaments.  His compassion is all-pervasive.</p>\n<p>2. <span style=\"color: #000080\">The Five Victorious Families who have gone beyond bliss</span>, aka The Five Buddha Families.</p>\n<p>3. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Chimé Tsukphü</span>.  He was the previous existence of the enlightened Lord Tönpa Shenrab Miwoche immediately before descending from heaven to earth.</p>\n<p>4. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Lhashen Yongsu Dakpa</span>.  He was the shen of the gods who received the teachings from Chimé Tsukphü.</p>\n<p>5. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Milu Samlek</span>.  He was the shen of the humans who received the teachings from Chime Tsukphü. He was born a prince in the ancient land of Tazik as his father, the king, was about to die.  He was known for his intelligence even as a young man.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: center\"><strong><span style=\"color: #000080\">#6-10  The Transmission of the Changeless Sages</span> </strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">6. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Lushen Yeshé Nyingpo</span>.  He is the shen of the <em>lu</em>, aka <em>nagas</em>, who received the teachings from Chime Tsukphü.</p>\n<p>7. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Ödzer Pakmé</span>.  He was a master from Tazik.</p>\n<p>8. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Münpo Künsal</span></p>\n<p>9. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Trulshen Nangden</span></p>\n<p>10. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Sangwa Düpa.  </span>He was a chief disciple of Chimé Tsukphü. He was born a prince of Tazik.</p>\n<div id=\"attachment_2727\" style=\"width: 491px\" class=\"wp-caption aligncenter\"><a href=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/tonggyung-thuchen-with-border.jpg?ssl=1\"><img data-recalc-dims=\"1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" aria-describedby=\"caption-attachment-2727\" data-attachment-id=\"2727\" data-permalink=\"https://ravencypresswood.com/2015/04/04/the-field-of-accumulation-the-lineage-of-the-three-renowned-cycles-of-dzogchen/tonggyung-thuchen-with-border/\" data-orig-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/tonggyung-thuchen-with-border.jpg?fit=792%2C1008&amp;ssl=1\" data-orig-size=\"792,1008\" data-comments-opened=\"0\" data-image-meta=\"{&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}\" data-image-title=\"\" data-image-description=\"\" data-image-caption=\"\" data-large-file=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/tonggyung-thuchen-with-border.jpg?fit=792%2C1008&amp;ssl=1\" class=\"  wp-image-2727\" src=\"https://i0.wp.com/ravencypresswood.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/04/tonggyung-thuchen-with-border.jpg?resize=481%2C613&#038;ssl=1\" alt=\"\" width=\"481\" height=\"613\" /></a><p id=\"caption-attachment-2727\" class=\"wp-caption-text\">Translator and great yogi, Lama Tonggyung Tuchen</p></div>\n<p style=\"text-align: center\"><strong><span style=\"color: #000080\">#11-14 The Transmission of the Scholarly Translators</span></strong></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">11. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Tonggyung Tüchen</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">12. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Sé Bön Shari Uchen </span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">13. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Chétsa Kharbu</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">14. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Nyachen Lishu Taring.  </span>A king and queen found a beautiful young girl in a forest. They named her Takza Liwer. Some time later, through the performance of ritual, she transformed herself into a man. However, she was still female when she wrote the root text for <em>The Three-fold Cycle of Dzogchen Proclamations</em> in the 8th century.  As a male, he became heir to the kingdom and was called Nyachen Lishu Taring.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">15. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Dé Bön Gyimtsa Machung  </span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">16. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Drupchen Chogye</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">17. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Choza Bönmo</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">18. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Zhotön Ngödrup Drakpa. </span>He was an emanation of Lishu Taring and a <em>tertön,</em> or treasure revealer,  who rediscovered hidden texts behind a statue of Vairochana in Lhodrak that were hidden by Lishu Taring during a time of persecution for the Yungdrung Bön.</p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">19. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Gyaton Jodé</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">20. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Zhikpo Nangseng</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">21. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Kyotön Ngakpal</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">22. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Yotön Lhunpal</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">23. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Sengé Drukdra</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">24. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Bönzhik Lodé</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">25. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Tokden Yungrin</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">26. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Khetsün Sherdrüp</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">27. <span style=\"color: #000080\">Yonten Sengé</span></p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left\">28. <span style=\"color: #000080\">The Second Victor and founder of Tashi Menri Monastery,</span> <span style=\"color: #000080\">Nyammé Sherap Gyalsten</span> <span style=\"color: #000080\">1356-1415.</span></p>\n", "protected": false}, "excerpt": {"rendered": "<p>In the Yungdrung Bön tradition, the place where the deities and objects of veneration are gathered is called the &#8220;Tsok Zhing&#8221;, the &#8220;Field of Accumulation&#8221;.  It is also sometimes translated as the &#8220;Field of Merit&#8221; or the &#8220;Merit Field&#8221; because what is being accumulated by paying homage and making offerings to this place is merit, or virtue.  [&hellip;]</p>\n", "protected": false}, "author": 44992789, "featured_media": 0, "comment_status": "closed", "ping_status": "open", "sticky": false, "template": "", "format": "image", "meta": {"_coblocks_attr": "", "_coblocks_dimensions": "", "_coblocks_responsive_height": "", "_coblocks_accordion_ie_support": "", "advanced_seo_description": "", "jetpack_seo_html_title": "", "jetpack_seo_noindex": false, "jetpack_seo_schema_type": "", "_wpcom_ai_launchpad_first_post": false, "_jetpack_feature_clip_id": 0, "_jetpack_memberships_contains_paid_content": false, "footnotes": ""}, "categories": [69608, 30524130, 5909569, 1, 295386984], "tags": [], "class_list": ["post-2717", "post", "type-post", "status-publish", "format-image", "hentry", "category-iconography", "category-prayer-and-ritual", "category-tibetan-lamas", "category-uncategorized", "category-zhang-zhung-nyen-gyu", "post_format-post-format-image"], "jetpack_likes_enabled": true, "jetpack_sharing_enabled": true, "jetpack_shortlink": "https://wp.me/p35YG8-HP", "jetpack-related-posts": [], "jetpack_featured_media_url": "", "_links": {"self": [{"href": "https://ravencypresswood.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts/2717", "targetHints": {"allow": ["GET"]}}], "collection": [{"href": "https://ravencypresswood.com/wp-json/wp/v